\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}Y,\\,z=5Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,\\frac{1}{2}Y,5Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105662/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105662"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection/20bee7ce4c894a8737cb5deda57012a20cb53ac6d14624e40e9546101a323639" :answer/id #uuid "a56189f9-f1a3-5391-bd91-16e1e6f643ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection/0eaa891be64e6034d28d1e3706998aac4200db766b80d512df5d5e76acd43a7e" :answer/id #uuid "fc8e2196-cd2d-5356-bf77-09c2b8be9228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection/45c264210ac2725d1a1da817dfe29f2d46e675f2dde574f02b22fa40930d2b71" :answer/id #uuid "82f3a81b-4486-5a7e-81a2-81f336324494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection/76a78d2e89274df8976009bb854f50476d94d5f1a7a8e77749da564812869cda" :answer/id #uuid "268b91a5-ed38-54af-a3c2-39060e86eba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection/69970300f7f1b2539764006f13b4e49afa2b273bfe07e50f9bb1b169d0a7f5ed" :answer/id #uuid "7325b847-a70b-5af1-a0f5-a981b3f7f805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d195ddf4-74ba-5cc6-ac50-e5851f570c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105660/selection/0eaa891be64e6034d28d1e3706998aac4200db766b80d512df5d5e76acd43a7e" :answer-field/choices ["q-105660/selection/20bee7ce4c894a8737cb5deda57012a20cb53ac6d14624e40e9546101a323639" "q-105660/selection/0eaa891be64e6034d28d1e3706998aac4200db766b80d512df5d5e76acd43a7e" "q-105660/selection/45c264210ac2725d1a1da817dfe29f2d46e675f2dde574f02b22fa40930d2b71" "q-105660/selection/76a78d2e89274df8976009bb854f50476d94d5f1a7a8e77749da564812869cda" "q-105660/selection/69970300f7f1b2539764006f13b4e49afa2b273bfe07e50f9bb1b169d0a7f5ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105660" :question/id #uuid "f1923526-f8b0-5c49-bd76-936fcf5d6c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-105660" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105660/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105660"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection/c4169e836ed05433c9fd03b9bb407eda9337da82adea9233031f9b37d2727608" :answer/id #uuid "eb2307d1-369a-5c08-82ed-2b05a829d9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection/31355a5b7a26321772b329ad94d865efedbf9f682bbc45b6d72a356f92c9c3a2" :answer/id #uuid "fa39841c-2dbb-5881-bbd1-0510f3d8d399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection/cd251c0a748722809936bf6b6965ed64c9838307127627939f176601b048f31e" :answer/id #uuid "7c7ff029-8f5b-522d-aab0-e844bc2e0803" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection/c6c25ff806ea7cef6d2fd94750856302239d96b6004d64910952a3b104ee8e15" :answer/id #uuid "303286a2-8e8c-575b-bfc8-44fea5756b12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection/910620df6f9902d0f4b15d55c01d26726f9b525bcdb4eee614706ac9b32af9a3" :answer/id #uuid "adf6c505-24d5-5bb2-bbda-63753da1523f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "308d7fef-c61c-5727-a963-73e5edbf67e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105689/selection/c4169e836ed05433c9fd03b9bb407eda9337da82adea9233031f9b37d2727608" :answer-field/choices ["q-105689/selection/c4169e836ed05433c9fd03b9bb407eda9337da82adea9233031f9b37d2727608" "q-105689/selection/31355a5b7a26321772b329ad94d865efedbf9f682bbc45b6d72a356f92c9c3a2" "q-105689/selection/cd251c0a748722809936bf6b6965ed64c9838307127627939f176601b048f31e" "q-105689/selection/c6c25ff806ea7cef6d2fd94750856302239d96b6004d64910952a3b104ee8e15" "q-105689/selection/910620df6f9902d0f4b15d55c01d26726f9b525bcdb4eee614706ac9b32af9a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105689" :question/id #uuid "f5674745-e1c6-5f88-ac0e-f59c0cf03027" :question/math-academy-id "q-105689" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+25{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=\\frac{1}{5}Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,2Y,\\frac{1}{5}Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105689/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb953f4-0a88-5d41-b62d-e8d7acd05801"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-103979"] :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" :answer/id #uuid "4aff7442-93fb-56f7-bff0-a8720b1a8ec8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" :answer/id #uuid "720c8284-2721-5421-9af7-b90ec2c69fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection/00e5a9633b225d28595eef377d7295907af8a777c6ac14b5df6d47bff03fb6fc" :answer/id #uuid "a77a817c-5081-574d-bd79-f85393947868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection/cb029ce3e517f18217d14d0fa5ce91cad80b26edfebd0fbe0b60ea218f163d4b" :answer/id #uuid "7028f727-56af-5ae1-89ce-61d4294db35e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection/2b572d0b6ef46a2c9ce9765c475325a5d52d4e158fef7efeda8b51f5dc29f2f9" :answer/id #uuid "c6d3b715-4c3a-5bc3-9bbf-ef03fae08ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cefd1bd8-b58d-5072-9751-9b723f63c8d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-103978/selection/2b572d0b6ef46a2c9ce9765c475325a5d52d4e158fef7efeda8b51f5dc29f2f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-103978/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" "q-103978/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" "q-103978/selection/00e5a9633b225d28595eef377d7295907af8a777c6ac14b5df6d47bff03fb6fc" "q-103978/selection/cb029ce3e517f18217d14d0fa5ce91cad80b26edfebd0fbe0b60ea218f163d4b" "q-103978/selection/2b572d0b6ef46a2c9ce9765c475325a5d52d4e158fef7efeda8b51f5dc29f2f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-103978" :question/id #uuid "e40041d8-fcdc-5773-bf4e-3e3bd1e930ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-103978" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "In cylindrical polar coordinates, the given half-cone has the equation\n\n$$\nz=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(x,y,z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-103978/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103978"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105726/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424e