 "0189da27-d830-59de-833d-5c14386ce8c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105726" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=3Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,Z,\\,3Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-105726/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105726"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-105722"] :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\,4Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection/2b572d0b6ef46a2c9ce9765c475325a5d52d4e158fef7efeda8b51f5dc29f2f9" :answer/id #uuid "c191af6c-8ef1-5003-9cb8-aa96913d3ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection/6fb15f04afb8f5bfe524ef5226af685290b083978309b529bc4d1d0612f39ebe" :answer/id #uuid "6d7a243b-d0fc-5328-9537-30a1c5a1103c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" :answer/id #uuid "f286c076-05a7-5735-98f6-f8ba54bbee4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection/90b3976e11d46a1c553555c26a46c86bdfe47ffa91fe76bec4990eda14af7864" :answer/id #uuid "8250f191-2d28-557f-a9c6-e7ac9eb5f40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection/4fe52bf7680412b43084df5c6080a7c0e255ad070020a9f4bab295141fad679a" :answer/id #uuid "069ad6d6-ea15-5d4e-b129-a9de8da70b4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{r}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9e3cde-3066-5e27-9fe0-8a02efedd981" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105725/selection/6fb15f04afb8f5bfe524ef5226af685290b083978309b529bc4d1d0612f39ebe" :answer-field/choices ["q-105725/selection/2b572d0b6ef46a2c9ce9765c475325a5d52d4e158fef7efeda8b51f5dc29f2f9" "q-105725/selection/6fb15f04afb8f5bfe524ef5226af685290b083978309b529bc4d1d0612f39ebe" "q-105725/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" "q-105725/selection/90b3976e11d46a1c553555c26a46c86bdfe47ffa91fe76bec4990eda14af7864" "q-105725/selection/4fe52bf7680412b43084df5c6080a7c0e255ad070020a9f4bab295141fad679a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105725" :question/id #uuid "2a0d0d94-b63d-5b48-bbbe-77b678514a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105725" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=4Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3X,\\,4Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-105725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "077dde84-b034-5b84-90c2-48e37def3295"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8883"] :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8882"] :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\:  5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad\ny =5 Y, \\qquad\nz = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\nY = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nZ = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,5Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8884"] :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad\ny = 6Y, \\qquad\nz = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,6Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
